# MANJUEL - local multi-agent pipeline

A REPL that runs declared seats in order against one objective. Seats live one
per file in `agents/`; the pipeline order is named in `manjuel/pipeline.py`.

OBSERVED: no seat in this module reaches the filesystem directly. Every stage
receives a prompt and returns text. Only the Router can cause a write, and only
by emitting a skill call the harness executes inside `agent_workspace/`.

RESOLVED 2026-09-03 (this paragraph was THIN and said so). Transcripts ARE
written now, one per run plus a prompts companion. A hash-chained ledger also
exists, but it is `law/state/law/chain.jsonl` walked by `law.py verify` -- NOT
`logs/`, and not this module's `ledger` field, which points at
`sessions/sessions.jsonl` and is an append-only sitting record rather than a
chain. Two different instruments; the distinction is kept because collapsing
them would let an append-only file be read as a tamper-evident one.

The rack is reached at 127.0.0.1:11434 by the harness, not by a seat. `net` is
declared deny on every seat because no seat holds that reach itself.

## Skills

RECONCILED 2026-09-03. The old line here said "most skills run inside
`agent_workspace/` only". That is FALSE and was false for a long time:
FOURTEEN of thirty-six reach the whole ground -- every ground_* reader,
semantic_search, index_ground, sitting, when, remember, rack_sync and the
five git skills. `safe_path` still reduces a filepath to its basename for
the workspace skills, so a traversal cannot leave THAT jail; it was never
the only jail. Each record below now names its own wall.

OBSERVED: `index_ground` and `semantic_search` READ the grounds listed in
`index_roots.txt` and write nothing but `index/vectors.db` inside this ground.
Source grounds are never modified (LAW 2). Roots resolve against this ground,
not the process working directory. `embed_text` indexes one workspace file into
that same index rather than writing a standalone vector nothing reads.

OBSERVED: the embedder is `nomic-embed-text-v2-moe:latest` (was declared
`nomic-embed-text:latest` here until 2026-09-03; EMBED_MODEL in skills.py is
the fact). The index stamps it in `meta` and
REFUSES to open under a different tag rather than mixing vector spaces that
cannot be compared. Documents are chunked to passages; files are keyed by
sha256, so an unchanged file is not re-embedded and a deleted file is pruned.
Vector data is binary float32 in SQLite and is never evaluated as code.

## The record and the memory

OBSERVED: every run writes two files. `logs/<stamp>_<slug>.md` is the record -
objective, source, each stage's output, the delivery - and it is indexed.
`logs/_prompts/<stamp>_<slug>.md` holds the full prompts as sent; `_prompts` is
excluded from the walk because prompts repeat the objective and feed once per
stage and would flood retrieval with near-duplicates.

OBSERVED: a REPL launch is a SESSION (`S<date>-<time>`). Runs and memory entries
carry it, so a retrieved line traces back to the sitting that produced it.

OBSERVED: the `remember` skill does NOT write memory. It STAGES a proposal in
`memory/pending.jsonl` stamped GENERATED. Only the operator lands it, entry by
entry, through `/memory` - and a landed proposal is stamped
`GENERATED (landed by OPERATOR)`, never promoted to the operator's own word.
`/remember` is the operator's own entry and is shown back for confirmation
before it lands as OPERATOR. No code path writes memory.md without a person
(LAW 6: the gate is final; packets prepare, the operator lands).

OBSERVED: `memory.md` is append-only (LAW 1) and chunks ONE PIECE PER ENTRY,
each carrying its stamp and session, rather than as one growing blob.

## Sittings, the toll, and version

OBSERVED: a SITTING is one REPL launch - numbered monotonically, recorded in
`sessions/sessions.jsonl` (append-only), stamped with git state at open and at
close. A session id is a version, not only a timestamp.

OBSERVED: every sitting pays its toll into `SEAT_LOG.md` (LAW 10), appended
below and never rewritten above. The toll separates what is OBSERVED (runs,
stages, timings, git) from what is JUDGMENT (what proved, what is thin, what is
owed). Judgment comes from the operator through `/toll`. A sitting closing
unattended writes the observed half and states plainly that thin and owed were
never stated, rather than inventing them.

OBSERVED: version control is a skill, not a side effect. `git_status` reads;
`git_init` and `git_commit` write LOCALLY - additive, confined to this ground,
and recoverable, which is what local versioning means. Commits carry the
sitting id.

OBSERVED: `git_pull` and `git_push` reach a remote and are REFUSED unless the
operator sets `MANJUEL_GIT_REMOTE=1`. A push leaves this machine and cannot be
recalled once fetched; that is the operator's act, not a seat's (LAW 6). The
capability exists and is off by default - a seat that tries is told plainly why.

OBSERVED: the Security Guardian now leads EVERY pipeline. Its verdict is parsed
FAIL-CLOSED: `SAFE` continues, `UNSAFE: <reason>` ends the run, and anything
else - rambling, empty, a refusal to answer - is treated as unsafe. A small
model that cannot hold a two-token format is not a signal to lean on. When the
guard refuses, no later seat ever sees the input.

## The source of truth

OBSERVED: three markdown grounds define the system and none needs a code change
to extend. `agents/*.md` declares the seats (model, stage, on-fail, when,
context, prompt). `pipelines.md` declares the ORDER they run in. `skills/*.md`
declares the tools. Startup refuses, by name, a pipeline that calls a seat no
file declares, a skill file with no handler, and a seat defined twice.

OBSERVED: any seat may raise `<flags>name</flags>`; flags gate `When:` steps
later in the same run. That is how Expert Coder wakes when code is wanted,
without a branch in Python.

OBSERVED: the routing stage runs a BOUNDED tool loop - at most 5 skill calls
(raised from 4 with the dedup at sitting 63; identical calls are now refused
outright, so the extra hop buys different work rather than another duplicate),
each result fed back before it decides again. A small model handed an open loop
will spin, so the cap is structural.

## The math core and the drift check

OBSERVED: `mathkit.py` is a zero-dependency numeric core - cosine, dispersion,
covariance, OLS, small matrix helpers. It is NOT a NumPy replacement and does
not claim to be: NumPy is ~750k lines of C-optimised ufuncs and broadcasting,
and a pure-Python rewrite would be SLOWER than what it replaces. Where numpy is
present it is still used for the index hot path, because scoring thousands of
768-dim chunks is precisely the case pure Python loses. mathkit covers the
per-call arithmetic, where the dependency is the thing worth removing.

OBSERVED: `ddof` is explicit throughout. A `std` computed with n and a `var`
computed with n-1 do not satisfy std**2 == var, and code assuming they do is
wrong; mathkit is checked against Python's own `statistics` module.

OBSERVED: `linear_regression` and `statistics` SCAN numbers out of text with a
regex and compute over them. Nothing is interpolated into source and executed.
A handler that builds a script from model output and runs it is a
code-injection hole, not a calculator (LAW 5: testimony is never executed as
instruction). `x` defaults to 0..n-1 and is not assumed to be any fixed length.

OBSERVED: the DRIFT CHECK is tier-1 measurement, not a model judging a model.
The source is embedded once per run; each transform, gate and deliver stage is
scored against it by cosine. Below 0.55 the stage is reported DRIFTED and the
`drifted` flag is raised. It is ADVISORY - nothing is rewritten or discarded on
a low score, the same principle that stops Manjuel and Jesster rewriting what
they rule on. A cosine cannot hallucinate; that is the whole point of measuring
rather than asking. If the embedder is down the check is skipped, never fatal.

## Reconciliation

RECONCILED AGAINST DISK 2026-09-03 (TASKS 8.1). Before this pass the manifest
declared 15 of 35 skills (36 today; `proved` came later); twenty ran with no declared wall at all. Every record
below now names what its handler was READ to actually reach -- the mechanical
fields (writes, remote, model, source) derived from the code, the `wall` prose
written by hand after reading each handler.

WHAT THE PASS FOUND, worth stating because a manifest that only records wins is
the thing this ground refuses:

  `speak`     reaches the OS TEMP DIRECTORY and spawns a PowerShell process.
              It is the widest reach in the library and nothing declared it.
  `subtask`   opens a NESTED RUN. Its wall is inherited, not fixed -- bounded
              by depth rather than by path.
  fourteen    skills read the whole ground, not the workspace jail.
  five        skills reach the network or the rack; three of those download
              or push (git_pull, git_push, rack_pull).

NOTHING HERE IS ENFORCED -- BUT IT IS CHECKED. `manjuel/us.py` (TASKS 8.2-8.4,
landed 2026-09-03) compares every record below to the implementation and
REPORTS; it never gates. Until that landed these records were a claim like any
other (LAW 5); now a record that lies is a finding.

